<header>
  <h1>🌤️ Why Is the Sky Blue? · 天空为什么是蓝色的?</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Three explanations for three audiences — English / 中文对照</p>
  <button id="toggle-zh" type="button">Toggle Chinese / 切换中文</button>
</header>

<!-- Section 1: Toddler -->
<section class="section">
  <h2>🧸 For a 3-Year-Old · 写给 3 岁的小朋友</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <article class="card en">
      <span class="lang-tag">ENGLISH</span>
      <p>You know how sunlight looks white? It's actually hiding a bunch of colors inside — like a rainbow!</p>
      <p>When sunlight comes down to Earth, it bumps into tiny little air bits all around us. The blue color in the light gets knocked around <em>everywhere</em> — it bounces and bounces all over the sky.</p>
      <p>So when you look up, your eyes catch all that bouncing blue light. That's why the sky looks blue! 🌤️</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card zh">
      <span class="lang-tag">简体中文</span>
      <p>你知道太阳光看起来是白色的吗?其实它里面藏着好多颜色——就像彩虹一样!</p>
      <p>当太阳光照到地球上时,它会撞上我们身边许许多多微小的空气颗粒。光里面的蓝色被<em>到处</em>撞来撞去——在天空中弹来弹去。</p>
      <p>所以当你抬起头,眼睛就会捕捉到这些到处乱弹的蓝光。天空就是这么变蓝的呀!🌤️</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: High school -->
<section class="section">
  <h2>🎒 For a High School Student · 写给高中生</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <article class="card en">
      <span class="lang-tag">ENGLISH</span>
      <p>Sunlight is made of all the colors of the visible spectrum, each with a different wavelength — red is long (~700 nm), violet/blue is short (~400 nm).</p>
      <p>When sunlight enters the atmosphere, it collides with nitrogen and oxygen molecules, which are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light. This causes <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, where the intensity of scattered light goes as:</p>
      <p class="formula">I ∝ <span class="frac"><span class="top">1</span><span class="bot">λ<sup>4</sup></span></span></p>
      <p>So shorter wavelengths scatter <em>way</em> more strongly — blue light (~450 nm) scatters roughly 5.5× more than red light (~700 nm). As sunlight passes through the atmosphere, the blue component gets scattered in all directions, so no matter where you look in the sky, some scattered blue light reaches your eyes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Why isn't the sky violet?</strong> Violet scatters even more than blue, but the Sun emits less violet, the upper atmosphere absorbs some of it, and our eyes are much less sensitive to violet. Blue wins out.</li>
        <li><strong>Why are sunsets red?</strong> At sunset, light travels through much more atmosphere. The blue light gets scattered out of the beam entirely before reaching you, leaving the longer-wavelength reds and oranges.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="card zh">
      <span class="lang-tag">简体中文</span>
      <p>太阳光由可见光谱中的所有颜色组成,每种颜色对应不同的波长——红光波长较长(约 700 纳米),紫/蓝光波长较短(约 400 纳米)。</p>
      <p>当太阳光进入大气层时,会与比可见光波长小得多的氮气和氧气分子发生碰撞,产生<strong>瑞利散射(Rayleigh scattering)</strong>,其中散射光的强度满足:</p>
      <p class="formula">I ∝ <span class="frac"><span class="top">1</span><span class="bot">λ<sup>4</sup></span></span></p>
      <p>因此波长越短的光散射越强烈——蓝光(约 450 纳米)的散射强度大约是红光(约 700 纳米)的 5.5 倍。当太阳光穿过大气层时,蓝光成分被向四面八方散射,所以无论你看天空的哪个方向,总有一些被散射的蓝光进入你的眼睛。</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>为什么天空不是紫色的?</strong>紫光的散射比蓝光更强,但太阳本身发出的紫光较少,高层大气也会吸收一部分紫光,而且我们人眼对紫光远不如对蓝光敏感。综合下来蓝色胜出。</li>
        <li><strong>为什么日落是红色的?</strong>日落时,阳光要穿过比正午长得多的路径才能到达你的眼睛。蓝光在途中就已被完全散射出光束,只剩下波长较长的红光和橙光。</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: PhD -->
<section class="section">
  <h2>🔬 For a Physics PhD · 写给物理学博士</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <article class="card en">
      <span class="lang-tag">ENGLISH</span>
      <p>The canonical explanation is Rayleigh scattering by atmospheric molecules in the dipole (long-wavelength) limit, where the size parameter x = 2πr/λ ≪ 1. The differential scattering cross-section for a small dielectric sphere (or equivalently, a molecule with polarizability α) is:</p>
      <p class="formula"><span class="frac"><span class="top">dσ</span><span class="bot">dΩ</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span class="top">k<sup>4</sup>α<sup>2</sup></span><span class="bot">16π<sup>2</sup>ε<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> (1 + cos<sup>2</sup>θ)</p>
      <p>yielding the familiar λ⁻⁴ dependence of the total cross-section, σ ∝ ν⁴. For dry air at STP, the volume scattering coefficient at 550 nm is on the order of ~10⁻⁵ m⁻¹, consistent with an atmospheric scattering mean free path of a few tens of kilometers — long enough that the direct solar beam remains largely unscattered (the Sun looks white at zenith) while the diffuse sky radiance is dominated by single scattering.</p>
      <p>A few refinements beyond the textbook version:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Depolarization and molecular anisotropy.</strong> Air molecules (N₂, O₂) have nonzero polarizability anisotropy, so Rayleigh scattered light is partially depolarized. The Cabannes correction (King factor, ~1.05 for air) modifies the pure-elastic treatment.</li>
        <li><strong>The violet question.</strong> The solar spectrum (~5800 K blackbody) declines below ~430 nm, stratospheric ozone Huggin bands absorb in the near-UV/violet, and S-cone sensitivity plus lens absorption suppress violet perception. The CIE chromaticity of diffuse skylight under single scattering lands in the blue, not violet.</li>
        <li><strong>Aerosols and multiple scattering.</strong> For accumulation-mode aerosols (r ~ 0.1–1 µm), Mie scattering applies, and the λ⁻⁴ dependence flattens to λ⁻¹ or weaker (Ångström exponent) — why hazy skies look whitish and horizon skies look paler.</li>
        <li><strong>Polarization.</strong> Single Rayleigh scattering is maximally polarized at 90° (the 1 + cos²θ phase function) — exploited by polarizing filters and polarization-navigation insects alike.</li>
        <li><strong>Beyond Earth.</strong> Full radiative-transfer treatments (Rayleigh + Mie phase functions, surface boundary conditions, multiple scattering via discrete ordinate methods) are what operational codes like DISORT or MODTRAN compute.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="card zh">
      <span class="lang-tag">简体中文</span>
      <p>标准的解释是:大气分子在偶极子(长波)极限下的瑞利散射,其中尺寸参数 x = 2πr/λ ≪ 1。对于小介质球(等价地,具有极化率 α 的分子),其微分散射截面为:</p>
      <p class="formula"><span class="frac"><span class="top">dσ</span><span class="bot">dΩ</span></span> = <span class="frac"><span class="top">k<sup>4</sup>α<sup>2</sup></span><span class="bot">16π<sup>2</sup>ε<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span> (1 + cos<sup>2</sup>θ)</p>
      <p>由此得到总截面熟悉的 λ⁻⁴ 依赖关系,σ ∝ ν⁴。对于标准状况下的干燥空气,550 nm 处的体积散射系数约为 10⁻⁵ m⁻¹ 量级,对应的大气散射平均自由程为几十公里——足够长,使得太阳直射光束基本不被散射(太阳在天顶时看起来是白色的),而漫射天空辐射则主要由单次散射主导。</p>
      <p>在教科书版本之外,还有几点更为精细的考量:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>退偏振与分子各向异性。</strong>空气分子(N₂、O₂)具有非零的极化率各向异性,因此瑞利散射光是部分退偏振的。卡巴尼斯修正(Cabannes correction,即金因子 King factor,空气约为 1.05)会对纯弹性处理作出修正。</li>
        <li><strong>紫光问题。</strong>太阳光谱(近似 5800 K 黑体)在约 430 nm 以下的输出逐渐下降,平流层臭氧的哈金斯带(Huggin bands)在近紫外/紫光波段有强吸收,再加上 S 锥灵敏度曲线与晶状体吸收的共同作用,压制了紫光的视觉感知。单次散射辐射传输下,漫射天空光的 CIE 色度落在蓝色区域,而非紫色。</li>
        <li><strong>气溶胶与多次散射。</strong>对于累积模态气溶胶(r ~ 0.1–1 µm),须采用米氏(Mie)散射,λ⁻⁴ 依赖关系变缓为 λ⁻¹ 甚至更弱(Ångström 指数)——这就是灰蒙蒙的天空显得发白、接近地平线的天空显得更淡的原因。</li>
        <li><strong>偏振。</strong>单次瑞利散射在 90° 散射角处偏振度最大(1 + cos²θ 相函数)——偏振滤镜和依靠偏振导航的昆虫都利用了这一事实。</li>
        <li><strong>地球之外。</strong>完整的辐射传输处理(瑞利相函数 + 气溶胶米氏相函数 + 地表边界条件 + 多次散射,例如离散纵标法)正是 DISORT 或 MODTRAN 等业务程序实际计算天空辐射亮度时所做的事。</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  <p><em>Sunlight + tiny molecules + λ⁻⁴ scattering = blue sky. · 阳光 + 微小分子 + λ⁻⁴ 散射 = 蓝色的天空。</em></p>
</footer>

